    <div class="panel" id="ak-port-juneau">
      <div class="sec"><div class="sh"><div class="st">🚁 Juneau, Alaska</div></div>
        <div id="daywx-ak-port-juneau"></div>
        <div class="tip-box"><div class="tip-lbl">Docked</div><div class="tip-txt">1:30 PM – 10:00 PM · Monday, Aug 17 (after Endicott Arm scenic cruising, 5:30–10:00 AM) · Your excursion: Mendenhall Glacier by Helicopter & Guided Walk, 4:45 PM</div></div>
        <div class="exc-item" style="margin-bottom:10px">
          <div class="exc-hdr" onclick="toggleExc(this)">
            <div class="exc-bar" style="background:#22d3ee"></div>
            <div class="exc-main"><div class="exc-day" style="color:#0891b2">🏔 Your Booked Excursion · 4:45 PM · ~2 hours</div><div class="exc-name">Mendenhall Glacier by Helicopter & Guided Walk 🚁🧊</div><div class="exc-meta">Order #195146282 · Guest: Larra</div></div>
            <div><div class="exc-price">$582.99</div></div>
            <div class="exc-chevron">▶</div>
          </div>
          <div class="exc-body">
            <div class="exc-desc">Board a modern turbine helicopter and lift off over lush rainforests, alpine ridges, and mountain peaks bordering Juneau. As you approach the icefield, you'll fly past twisted spires, deep blue crevasses, and the jagged 7,000-ft Mendenhall Towers before a gentle landing directly on the glacier's surface. From there, your expert glacier guide leads a walk across the ice — exploring icefalls, crevasses, and the hanging glaciers suspended on the surrounding mountains, while explaining how this 13-mile-long, half-mile-wide river of ice has been slowly retreating since the mid-1700s. The afternoon departure (4:45 PM) works perfectly after the morning's scenic cruising through Endicott Arm.</div>
            <div style="margin-top:10px"><div class="exc-full-toggle" onclick="event.stopPropagation();toggleExcFull(this)"><span>📄 Full Excursion Description</span><span class="exc-full-chev">▶</span></div><div class="exc-full-body"><div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ts);line-height:1.65;padding:8px 2px 2px">Your pilot takes you up over alpine ridges and rainforest, narrating as you approach the Juneau Icefield. You'll pass close to the jagged Mendenhall Towers — rock spires that rise more than 7,000 feet — before a gentle landing directly on the moving ice of Mendenhall Glacier. An expert glacier guide then leads you on foot across the glacier's surface, explaining how it forms and flows and pointing out its blue crevasses and icefalls. You'll spend roughly 20–25 minutes actually on the ice before reboarding for the flight back — total flight time is about 30 minutes each way, so plan on about 2 hours door to door.</div><div style="font-size:10px;color:var(--tm);margin-top:6px;font-style:italic">Sourced from the tour operator's listing — Celebrity's own excursion page requires signing in to your reservation to view.</div></div></div>
            <div class="tip-box" style="margin-top:9px"><div class="tip-lbl">What to wear 🧥</div><div class="tip-txt">
              <strong>How cold:</strong> Downtown Juneau in mid-August usually runs high-50s to mid-60s°F. The glacier surface itself is typically 5–10°F colder than downtown — so realistically <strong>high 30s to low 50s°F</strong> on the ice, and it can feel colder still once you add wind off the icefield and rotor wash. Glacier boots that strap over your own shoes are provided at the heliport, but everything else is on you.<br><br>
              <strong>Layer it like this:</strong><br>
              • <strong>Base:</strong> moisture-wicking long-sleeve top (skip cotton — it stays wet and cold)<br>
              • <strong>Mid:</strong> fleece or a warm sweater<br>
              • <strong>Outer:</strong> waterproof, windproof jacket — this is the layer doing the real work against the wind<br>
              • <strong>Bottoms:</strong> real pants (not shorts/leggings alone) — jeans are fine for the ~20–25 min on ice, water-resistant hiking pants if you have them<br>
              • <strong>Accessories:</strong> warm hat or beanie, gloves, sunglasses (glare off the ice is real)<br>
              • <strong>Shoes:</strong> sturdy closed-toe shoes you don't mind getting wet (the provided glacier boots strap over these) — no sandals or slip-ons<br><br>
              No loose scarves near the rotors. Body-attached camera straps only — nothing loose gets on the helicopter.</div></div>
            <div class="exc-links"><a class="exc-link" href="https://www.helicopteradventures.com" target="_blank">Helicopter Adventures ↗</a><a class="exc-link" href="https://www.nps.gov/glba/learn/nature/mendenhall.htm" target="_blank">About Mendenhall ↗</a></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="card hi" style="margin-bottom:8px">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:800;margin-bottom:6px">👀 What to See</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ts);line-height:1.7">
            <strong>Mendenhall Glacier</strong> — you'll see it from the air on your helicopter excursion, one of the most photographed glaciers in Alaska.<br>
            <strong>Mount Roberts Tramway</strong> — a quick ride up for panoramic views over the Gastineau Channel and downtown Juneau.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="card hi" style="margin-bottom:8px">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:800;margin-bottom:6px">🍽️ What to Try</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ts);line-height:1.7">Fresh halibut and salmon from the waterfront cafes, and a stop at Alaskan Brewing's tasting room if you have time before the ship departs.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="card hi" style="border-left:4px solid #b8860b">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:800;margin-bottom:6px">🥃 Speakeasy Picks</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ts);line-height:1.7">
            <strong>The Alaskan Hotel & Bar</strong> — opened in 1913, an actual Prohibition-era speakeasy with real history (and a reputation for being haunted).<br>
            <strong>The Narrows Bar</strong> — modern speakeasy-style craft cocktails; ask for a pour over their 1,000-year-old iceberg ice.
          </div>
