        <div class="tip-box"><div class="tip-txt">What Mandarin Oriental <em>does</em> have going for it food-wise: it's genuinely close to Charles Bridge and the Malá Strana dining scene generally, and its own restaurant (Essensia/Spices, per recent reviews) is well-regarded if you want a low-effort excellent dinner without crossing the river at all.</div></div>


        
        

        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:800;color:var(--ac);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.06em;margin:16px 0 6px">🍽️ Prague's Michelin-Starred Restaurants</div>
        <div class="tip-box" style="border-color:#22c55e;background:rgba(34,197,94,.08)"><div class="tip-lbl" style="color:#15803d">🤔 How to pick your 2</div><div class="tip-txt">You have 2 open evenings — here's the fastest way to choose: <strong>La Degustation</strong> if you want the single most iconic "why Prague has Michelin stars" meal (game, tradition, 3-hour formal experience). <strong>Levitate</strong> if you want the wildest, most memorable story to tell afterward (18-course mystery menu, priciest of the five). <strong>Casa De Carli</strong> if you want warmth and personality over pure ceremony (family-run, only 25 seats). <strong>Field</strong> is the easy crowd-pleaser — excellent, slightly more relaxed, still a serious tasting menu. <strong>Štangl</strong> is the one for a genuinely different, foraged-Czech-countryside experience if you're willing to take a short taxi. A strong combo: one "classic" night (La Degustation or Field) + one "adventurous" night (Levitate) covers both ends of the spectrum.</div></div>
        <div class="tip-box"><div class="tip-txt">Prague has 5 one-star restaurants as of the 2026 Michelin Guide (all real, current — verified this year). Distances below are walking time from the Marriott at V Celnici 8. Book 2-3 weeks ahead minimum; smart-casual dress is fine everywhere, no jacket required.</div></div>
        <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">La Degustation Bohême Bourgeoise ⭐</div><div class="rc-type">Modern Czech · Haštalská 18, Old Town · ~28 min walk or ~10 min taxi from Mandarin Oriental · ~140-260 EUR/person</div><div class="rc-desc">This is the restaurant that proved Czech cuisine belongs on the world stage. Chef Oldřich Sahajdák built the entire concept around a rediscovered 1894 Czech cookbook by Marie B. Svobodová — in 2012 it became the first restaurant to win a Michelin star for Czech cuisine specifically, and it's defended that star every year since. Forbes Life called it "the first choice for its buckle-in, three-hour seven-course tasting menus." Expect an open kitchen, golden ceiling, and a glass-walled wine cave. Past tasting courses have included juniper-marinated fallow deer in a beef-beetroot-juniper jus, and caramelized onion with pickled sprouts and pear purée — game and foraged ingredients are the signature. Three menu paths: Czech traditional, Czech-fusion, and pan-European, which can be combined. <strong>~3,500-5,500 CZK per person (~$165-260)</strong> depending on menu length.</div><div class="rc-tip">💡 Book via ladegustation.cz — 2-3 weeks out minimum. If you want just one truly "this is why Prague has Michelin stars" meal, this is the one.</div><div class="rc-tip">⭐ 4.5/5 (Restaurant Guru, 3,421 reviews) — consistently among the most-praised meals visitors have anywhere in Prague. <strong>Order:</strong> pork-fat poached catfish, and the fallow deer if it's on the seasonal menu — nose-to-tail dishes like chicken hearts, lamb sweetbreads, and beef tongue are the house specialty. <strong>Drink:</strong> skip wine once and try the juice pairing instead — reviewers consistently call it out as genuinely inventive, not a consolation option.</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=La+Degustation+Boheme+Bourgeoise+Hastalska+18+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.ladegustation.cz/en" target="_blank">Website ↗</a><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurant_Review-g274707-d1096547-Reviews-La_Degustation_Boheme_Bourgeoise-Prague_Bohemia.html" target="_blank">Reviews ↗</a></div>
        <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Field ⭐</div><div class="rc-type">Farm-to-table Modern · U Milosrdných 12, Old Town · ~25 min walk or ~9 min taxi from Mandarin Oriental · ~5,400 CZK (~$254) for the 10-course</div><div class="rc-desc">A genuine underdog story — Field won its Michelin star after just its first year open, which almost never happens. Chef Radek Kašpárek's whole philosophy is "food in its original, unadorned form" — ingredients sourced directly from named Czech farmers, seasonal menus that change constantly, and dishes that sometimes go all the way back to half-forgotten local staples like marrow or swede alongside more exotic touches like sea fish. Two tasting options: a shorter 6-course or the full 10-course experience (~5,400 CZK, roughly $254), plus a 4-course lunch menu available until 3pm for a lighter/cheaper daytime option.</div><div class="rc-tip">💡 A touch more relaxed and less formal than La Degustation, but still very serious cooking — good pick if you want excellent food without the full 3-hour formal experience.</div><div class="rc-tip">⭐ Solid reviews, though a smaller/newer footprint than La Degustation. <strong>Order:</strong> the full 10-course tasting if you have the appetite and time — reviewers specifically call out the arctic char with braised skin as a standout. <strong>Drink:</strong> wine pairing "A" is poured via Coravin, a nice touch that keeps pours precise and fresh even on rarer bottles.</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Field+Restaurant+U+Milosrdnych+12+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.fieldrestaurant.cz/en" target="_blank">Website ↗</a><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurant_Review-g274707-d7235557-Reviews-Field_Restaurant-Prague_Bohemia.html" target="_blank">Reviews ↗</a></div>
        <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Casa De Carli ⭐</div><div class="rc-type">Modern Italian · Vězeňská 5, Old Town · ~26 min walk or ~9 min taxi from Mandarin Oriental · mains ~$16-24, tasting menu likely ~$120-165</div><div class="rc-desc">The most personal of the five — a genuine family restaurant. Chef Matteo De Carli and his wife Lenka Hermanová have run it together since 2012, and much of the produce comes from their own garden near Mělník. It channels North Italian home cooking elevated to Michelin level — signature dishes include spaghetti cacio e pepe with beef tartare, housemade ravioli, chestnut soup, and a tableside tiramisu. There's a chef's table for up to 8 in the basement where Matteo cooks right in front of you. Only 25 seats total, and both à la carte and a 5, 6, or 7-course tasting menu are available.</div><div class="rc-tip">💡 Smallest room of the five — book earlier than the others given limited seats. Best pick if you want warmth and personality over pure formality.</div><div class="rc-tip">⭐ 4.5/5 (Restaurant Guru, 1,170 reviews) — reviews split slightly on value at the top end (a few call it pricey for what's delivered), but service and atmosphere get consistent praise. <strong>Order:</strong> the turbot in puff pastry crust if it's on the daily specials, plus the housemade ravioli and spaghetti cacio e pepe with beef tartare — signature dishes reviewers return for. <strong>Drink:</strong> ask to see the walk-in wine fridge wall and let the sommelier guide you — the Moravian-Italian list is a real strength here.</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Casa+De+Carli+Vezenska+5+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.casadecarli.cz/en" target="_blank">Website ↗</a><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurant_Review-g274707-d3434764-Reviews-Casa_De_Carli_Ristorante-Prague_Bohemia.html" target="_blank">Reviews ↗</a></div>
        <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Levitate ⭐</div><div class="rc-type">Asian-Nordic Fusion · Štěpánská 14, New Town · ~30 min walk or ~12 min taxi from Mandarin Oriental · ~7,500 CZK (~$353) for the 18-course, less for the 12-course</div><div class="rc-desc">The wild card of the group, and the newest star (first awarded in 2025). Chef-owner Christian "Chris" Chu founded it in 2017 drawing on his own Asian roots blended with Nordic technique, all built on Czech ingredients. The menu is structured in three chapters — Aqua (seafood), Fauna (meat), Flora (vegetables/fruit) — including a course guests cook alongside Chu himself. The signature Fauna dish pairs Wagyu with duck in five spices. Choose the 12-course "Elite" menu or go all-in on the 18-course "Mystery" menu, where you get zero advance notice of what's coming — genuinely rare in fine dining. A 1,500 CZK/person deposit is required to book, and it's the priciest of the five (~$353 for the full 18-course).</div><div class="rc-tip">💡 The most experimental/adventurous of the five — pick this if you want a genuine "we've never had anything like this" story, not classic fine dining. No à la carte, no kids' menu, and the room isn't wheelchair accessible.</div><div class="rc-tip">⭐ Genuinely mixed reviews — worth knowing before you book. Many reviewers call it magnificent and worth every penny ("a very promising young chef"), but there's a real minority who found the 18-course "Mystery" menu overpriced for the portions and execution, especially at ~$690 for two. <strong>Order:</strong> if available, the sturgeon gets specifically praised, and the signature Fauna course (Wagyu + duck, five spices) is the dish built around Chu's own style. <strong>Drink:</strong> the wine/juice pairing is well-regarded — go with the pairing rather than ordering off the wine list solo, since several reviews suggest the individual bottle selection undersells the food.</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Levitate+Restaurant+Stepanska+14+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://levitate.cz" target="_blank">Website ↗</a><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurant_Review-g274707-d12645464-Reviews-LEVITATE-Prague_Bohemia.html" target="_blank">Reviews ↗</a></div>
        <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Štangl ⭐</div><div class="rc-type">Modern Czech · Pernerova 49, Karlín · ~40 min walk or ~15 min taxi from Mandarin Oriental · tasting menu likely ~$120-190</div><div class="rc-desc">The most "rooted in the land" of the five. Chef Martin Štangl built on the philosophy he helped establish at Prague's beloved Eska bakery before splitting off into his own restaurant. The 3 or 5-course menus lean hard into foraged and half-forgotten Czech ingredients — wild herbs, forest finds, things you genuinely won't find on any other menu in the city. It also holds a Michelin <strong>Green Star</strong> specifically for sustainable sourcing and practices, on top of its regular star. Farthest of the five from both hotels, tucked into the up-and-coming Karlín neighborhood across the river.</div><div class="rc-tip">💡 Worth the short taxi ride — this is the pick if you want the most distinctly "Czech countryside" experience of the group, rather than something international-influenced.</div><div class="rc-tip">⭐ Reviews are positive overall ("absolutely wonderful food, wine, and overall experience"), with one recurring theme: portions on the tasting menus run smaller than expected for the price, and wine pairing communication has occasionally been a friction point — confirm what's included in your pairing package before you order. <strong>Order:</strong> whatever's built around the day's foraged ingredients — the menu leans into wild herbs and forest finds rather than a fixed signature dish, so ask your server what's freshest that day. <strong>Drink:</strong> the "old world" wine pairing option is the one to get if you want traditional pairings (though request red with meat courses explicitly if that's your preference).</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Stangl+Restaurant+Pernerova+49+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://stangl.ambi.cz" target="_blank">Website ↗</a><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurant_Review-g274707-d26778366-Reviews-Stangl-Prague_Bohemia.html" target="_blank">Reviews ↗</a></div>

        <div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:800;color:var(--ac);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.06em;margin:16px 0 6px">🏰 Things to Do — Distance from Mandarin Oriental</div>
        <div class="tip-box"><div class="tip-txt">Mandarin Oriental sits in Malá Strana, right at the base of the castle district — Prague Castle and Charles Bridge are essentially on your doorstep, while Old Town/New Town sights are a bit farther.</div></div>
        <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Charles Bridge</div><div class="rc-type">~5 min walk — right there</div><div class="rc-desc">This is Mandarin Oriental's single biggest advantage — you're essentially living next to it, easy to visit early morning or late at night on a whim.</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Charles+Bridge+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a></div>
        <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Prague Castle & St. Vitus Cathedral</div><div class="rc-type">~10-12 min walk (uphill)</div><div class="rc-desc">Dramatically closer than from the Marriott (~30 min there vs. ~10-12 here) — this is the other half of Mandarin's location advantage. Plan 3-4 hours; go early to beat tour groups.</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Prague+Castle" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a></div>
        <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Vltava River Cruise</div><div class="rc-type">~5-8 min walk to docks</div><div class="rc-desc">An hour-long boat cruise past Charles Bridge and Prague Castle is one of the best low-effort ways to see the city, especially lit up in the evening. Closer from here since Mandarin sits right on the riverfront.</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Vltava+river+cruise+dock+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a></div>
        <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Old Town Square & Astronomical Clock</div><div class="rc-type">~15-18 min walk (across Charles Bridge)</div><div class="rc-desc">The heart of Prague — Gothic Church of Our Lady before Týn, the medieval Astronomical Clock, cafes lining the square. Charles Bridge itself is the route there, so still a pleasant walk despite the distance.</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Old+Town+Square+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a></div>
        <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Jewish Quarter (Josefov)</div><div class="rc-type">~20-22 min walk</div><div class="rc-desc">One of Europe's best-preserved Jewish quarters — Old Jewish Cemetery, Old-New Synagogue, Spanish Synagogue all within a few blocks, conveniently near Field and Casa De Carli if combining with dinner.</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Josefov+Jewish+Quarter+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a></div>
        <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Wenceslas Square</div><div class="rc-type">~25-28 min walk or short taxi</div><div class="rc-desc">The biggest gap of the six from this side — a broad boulevard anchored by the National Museum. Right by Levitate if dining there.</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Wenceslas+Square+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a></div>

        <details style="margin-bottom:10px;margin-top:10px">
          <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;color:var(--ac);padding:10px 12px;background:var(--acb);border-radius:10px;border:2px dashed var(--b)">▸ If staying at the Marriott instead — same 6 sights, different distances (tap to expand)</summary>
          <div style="margin-top:8px">
          <div class="tip-box"><div class="tip-txt">This flips the picture — the Marriott sits in the walkable Old Town/New Town core, so Old Town Square, the Jewish Quarter, and Wenceslas Square get much closer, while Prague Castle and Charles Bridge get farther.</div></div>
          <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Old Town Square & Astronomical Clock</div><div class="rc-type">~8 min walk</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Old+Town+Square+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a></div>
          <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Charles Bridge</div><div class="rc-type">~15 min walk</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Charles+Bridge+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a></div>
          <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Prague Castle & St. Vitus Cathedral</div><div class="rc-type">~30 min walk or ~10 min taxi/tram</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Prague+Castle" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a></div>
          <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Jewish Quarter (Josefov)</div><div class="rc-type">~10 min walk</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Josefov+Jewish+Quarter+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a></div>
          <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Wenceslas Square</div><div class="rc-type">~12 min walk</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Wenceslas+Square+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a></div>
          <div class="rc"><div class="rc-name">Vltava River Cruise</div><div class="rc-type">~15 min walk to docks</div><a class="rc-lnk" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Vltava+river+cruise+dock+Prague" target="_blank">View on map ↗</a></div>
          </div>
        </details>
        <div class="tip-box" style="border-color:#0891b2;background:rgba(8,145,178,.08)"><div class="tip-txt">The pattern: Mandarin Oriental trades Old Town/New Town convenience for being right on top of Prague's two most iconic sights (Castle + Charles Bridge). If those two are your priority photo/experience of the trip, that's a real point in Mandarin's favor even beyond the atmosphere argument already noted above.</div></div>

