        <div class="tip-box" style="border-color:#f59e0b;background:rgba(245,158,11,.08)"><div class="tip-lbl" style="color:#92400e">⏱️ Flight Times — No Direct Flights Exist on Either Route</div><div class="tip-txt">Worth knowing before you search: <strong>there are no nonstop flights from IAD to Budapest, and none from Prague to IAD either</strong> — every option requires at least one connection.<br><br>
        <strong>One interesting fact:</strong> Austrian Airlines flies IAD → Vienna <em>nonstop</em> (8h 15m) — Vienna isn't your destination, but it's worth knowing this exists if a Budapest routing through Vienna ever looks appealing.<br><br>
        <strong>If a direct flight existed</strong> (it doesn't, this is just for reference): based on distance, IAD→BUD would be roughly <strong>8h 15-30m</strong> nonstop (almost identical to the confirmed Austrian IAD→Vienna time, since Budapest and Vienna are only ~250 km apart), and PRG→IAD would be roughly <strong>7h 50m-8h</strong>. So the real one-stop options above are adding roughly <strong>3.5-4.5 hours</strong> on top of the theoretical direct time once realistic layovers are factored in — that's the actual cost of the required connection.<br><br>
        Since a stop is unavoidable either way, prioritize a <strong>short, familiar connecting hub</strong> (Frankfurt, Amsterdam, Vienna) over total flight count — a well-timed 1-stop often beats a technically-fewer-stops option with a long layover.</div></div>

        <div class="card hi" style="margin-bottom:10px">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:800;margin-bottom:6px">✈️ IAD → BUD — Segment-by-Segment (realistic, ~12-12.5 hrs total)</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ts);line-height:1.8">
          <strong>Via Amsterdam (KLM):</strong> IAD→AMS ~7h 45m, layover ~1.5-2.5h, AMS→BUD ~2h<br>
          <strong>Via Frankfurt (Lufthansa):</strong> IAD→FRA ~7h 50m, layover ~1.5-2h, FRA→BUD ~1h 35m<br>
          <strong>Via Vienna (Austrian):</strong> IAD→VIE nonstop 8h 15m, then a short VIE→BUD hop ~50 min — but still needs ~1.5-2h layover in Vienna to clear immigration and change terminals, so this doesn't actually save much time overall despite the nonstop first leg
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="card hi" style="margin-bottom:14px">
          <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:800;margin-bottom:6px">✈️ PRG → IAD — Segment-by-Segment (realistic, ~11.5-12 hrs total)</div>
          <div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ts);line-height:1.8">
          <strong>Via Reykjavik (Icelandair) — shortest overall:</strong> PRG→KEF ~3h 40m, layover ~1.5-2h, KEF→IAD ~5h 30m<br>
          <strong>Via Amsterdam (KLM):</strong> PRG→AMS ~1h 35m, layover ~1.5-2.5h, AMS→IAD ~9h 15m<br>
          <strong>Via Frankfurt (Lufthansa):</strong> PRG→FRA ~1h, layover ~1.5-2h, FRA→IAD ~9h 30m
          </div>
          <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--tm);margin-top:6px">Note: PRG→IAD clears US immigration/customs on arrival at IAD itself (not at the connecting hub), so the connecting-hub layover here is purely Schengen-to-Schengen — genuinely can run closer to 1-1.5h at a well-organized airport, unlike the outbound direction.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="tip-box" style="border-color:#f59e0b;background:rgba(245,158,11,.08)"><div class="tip-txt">🛂 <strong>Why layovers run long westbound-into-Europe:</strong> flying IAD→(hub)→BUD means clearing EU immigration/passport control at the FIRST European stop, before your connecting flight — that's on top of normal gate-to-gate walking time, and at a big hub like Amsterdam or Frankfurt it realistically eats 45-90 min by itself. 1-1.5h total layover is the airline's bare minimum booking window, not a comfortable real-world buffer — 1.5-2.5h is the honest range once you factor in a slow immigration line or a delayed inbound flight.</div></div>
          <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--tm);margin-top:6px">These are typical scheduled block times for common routings — actual figures shift by exact date/carrier, but this gives a realistic sense of what each option feels like.</div>
